import type { TimeBlockContent } from '../simulation'

// Management Consulting: Public Sector scenario (Project Gateway).
// A 10-week operational review of a state human-services agency benefits backlog.
// Day-in-the-Life (7 blocks, county field day) + Full Simulation (29 blocks, Sun-Fri).
// Prose copied verbatim from consulting-publicsector-*-V1.md source drafts.
// Cast: Renata (EM), David Osei (Partner), Theo (2nd-yr), Frank Delgado (deputy),
// Commissioner Angela Reyes, Wanda Pruitt (county manager), Marisol (caseworker).
export const managementConsultantPublicSectorContent: Record<string, TimeBlockContent> = {
  "management-consultant-public-sector-dil-d1-b1": {
    "before": "Most of this engagement has happened in a conference room at DHS headquarters in the state capital, staring at a dataset of processing times for 38 counties. Today is different. You and Theo are driving 90 minutes out to Eastwood County, one of the worst-performing offices in the state, to see the backlog with your own eyes. The numbers told you Eastwood is slow. They did not tell you why. That is the point of today.",
    "simulatedWork": "Theo drives. You have the dataset open on your laptop in the passenger seat, and you spend the first twenty minutes walking him through what you are bringing into the room. Eastwood takes a median of 47 days to process a Medicaid application. The fastest county in the state, a similar-sized rural office two hours north, takes 9. Same rules, same forms, same software, same federal mandate. A five-fold difference that nobody at headquarters can fully explain.\n\nTheo has done a dozen of these field days and gives you the unwritten rules for the drive. \"We are guests, not auditors. Wanda has had three consulting firms walk through her office in eight years and watched all of them leave without changing anything. She is going to assume we are the fourth. Our entire job this morning is to be the team that actually listened.\" He pauses. \"And do not lead with the dataset. If you walk in and say 'your office is the slowest in the state,' the conversation is over. She already knows her numbers. She wants to know if you understand why.\"\n\nYou close the laptop and look out the window for the last half hour. The capital's office parks give way to farmland, then to a county seat that has clearly seen better decades: a shuttered department store, a courthouse, and a low brick building with a line of people already formed outside the door at 8:20 in the morning, forty minutes before it opens.",
    "commentary": "Notice what Theo did, because it is the whole craft of a field visit compressed into a car ride. He reframed you from auditor to guest, told you to lead with curiosity rather than the damning number, and made the human relationship the priority over the analysis. This is not him being soft. It is him being effective. In commercial work you sometimes get away with walking in cold and letting the data do the talking. In a government office where a career manager has watched consultants come and go, the data is worthless until the person trusts you enough to explain what is behind it. The line outside the door before opening is your first real data point of the day, and it is not in any spreadsheet.",
    "after": "You park across from the brick building. The line is now maybe thirty people, some with kids, a few in folding chairs they clearly brought from home. Theo cuts the engine. \"That line,\" he says, \"is the thing we are actually here about. Try not to forget it when we are back in the conference room arguing about medians.\" You go in."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-dil-d1-b2": {
    "before": "Wanda meets you at a side door before the public entrance opens. She is brisk, polite, and visibly braced for another firm's visit. She gives you fifteen minutes to walk the floor before the doors open at 9, which you understand is both a courtesy and a test.",
    "simulatedWork": "The office is smaller than you pictured and more worn. Eleven caseworker desks, most piled with manila folders in leaning stacks. A waiting area with maybe twenty chairs, which you now know will not be close to enough. A single intake window. A back room where Wanda gestures at a row of filing cabinets and a fax machine that is, astonishingly, running.\n\nYou do what Theo coached: you ask questions and you shut up. \"Walk me through what happens when someone in that line reaches the front.\" Wanda does, and the picture assembles fast. An applicant arrives, often having taken a half-day off hourly work to be here, often for the second or third time because something in their paperwork was incomplete. They take a number. A caseworker pulls their file, or starts one. Eligibility for Medicaid and SNAP requires verifying income, household size, residency, and now, under the rules coming in 14 weeks, work hours. Each verification can require a document the applicant does not have on them, which means a second trip, which means the application sits in a stack marked \"pending\" for days or weeks until the document arrives, if it ever does.\n\nYou start to see the five-fold gap forming in front of you, not as a number but as a physical thing: stacks. Eastwood runs on paper and second trips. You ask Wanda how many of her eleven desks are actually staffed today. She says seven. Four positions are vacant; she has been trying to fill them for a year, and the ones she does hire leave within months for jobs that pay more and do not involve being yelled at by desperate people. Turnover, you realize, is not a footnote to the throughput problem. It might be the throughput problem.\n\nAt 9:00 the doors open and the room fills. You step to the side and watch the first twenty minutes of the day's intake. It is quieter than you expected, and somehow harder to watch for it.",
    "commentary": "This is the heart of why field visits exist. The dataset said \"Eastwood: 47-day median.\" The floor says \"seven of eleven desks staffed, paper-based verification, applicants making multiple trips, chronic turnover driven by pay and stress.\" The number is the symptom; the floor is the disease. A consultant who only ever sees the spreadsheet will recommend something clean and wrong, like a new dashboard or a process tweak. A consultant who walks the floor sees that the real levers are staffing, verification friction, and the human cost of second trips. Also notice you are forming a hypothesis (turnover may be the core driver) but holding it loosely, as one input, not a conclusion. You have seen one office. One office is an anecdote, not a finding. The discipline is to let the floor generate the hypothesis and the data confirm or kill it.",
    "after": "Wanda has to run the floor now. She hands you off to one of her veteran caseworkers, Marisol, who has agreed to let you sit beside her for a while, and to talk to you on her break. This is the part of the day where the abstraction fully dissolves."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-dil-d1-b3": {
    "before": "Marisol has worked this office for eleven years. She processes more applications than anyone on the floor, and Wanda quietly told you she is the reason Eastwood is not even worse. You pull a chair beside her desk, notebook out, and mostly watch.",
    "simulatedWork": "The first thing you notice is how much of Marisol's job is not eligibility judgment but document chasing. A young mother's application has been pending for three weeks, waiting on a pay stub the system requires to verify income. Marisol calls her, gets voicemail, leaves a message she has clearly left before. \"She works two jobs,\" Marisol says, not looking up. \"She has been in twice. The pay stub she brought was the wrong month. It is nobody's fault and it is going to take her another trip.\" She flags the file and moves it back to the pending stack, which is the tallest stack on her desk.\n\nYou start counting silently. In the first hour, Marisol touches maybe fifteen files. She completes three. The other twelve go back to pending, each one waiting on a document, a callback, a system that times out, or a verification step that cannot be finished today. You begin to understand the 47-day median in your gut. It is not that the work is slow. It is that almost nothing can be finished in one sitting, so everything cycles through the pending stack multiple times, and each cycle adds days.\n\nYou ask Marisol the question you have been holding. \"If you could change one thing about how this works, what would it be?\" She thinks for a real moment. \"Let me verify income electronically. The state has the wage data. It is sitting in another agency's system. If I could check a box and pull it instead of waiting for a human being who works two jobs to find the right pay stub and come back here a third time, I would clear half my pending stack by Friday.\" She says it flatly, like someone who has said it before to people who nodded and left.\n\nYou write it down word for word. Theo, sitting two desks away pretending to read a procedures binder, catches your eye and gives the smallest nod. You both heard the same thing: the person doing the work just named the highest-leverage fix in the building, and it is not a consulting framework. It is data-matching the agency could already do.",
    "commentary": "Two things to carry out of this block. First, the frontline worker is almost always the cheapest, richest source of insight on the whole engagement, and almost always the most overlooked. Marisol just handed you a finding that no dashboard would have surfaced: the pending-stack cycle is the throughput killer, and electronic income verification could collapse it. Senior people at headquarters may not know this in their bones because they have never sat at this desk. Second, watch how you took the quote verbatim. In a few days, in a steering committee room, \"let me verify income electronically and I clear half my stack by Friday\" from a real eleven-year caseworker will land harder than any chart you could build. The data proves the pattern; the voice from the floor makes a commissioner feel it. You need both.",
    "after": "Marisol's break is at 11. She has agreed to spend it talking to you and Theo about what the last few years have actually been like, which is the conversation you most need and least want to rush."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-dil-d1-b4": {
    "before": "You and Theo sit with Marisol in a cramped break room. No notebook out at first, just listening. This is the conversation that will not show up in any model but will quietly shape everything you recommend.",
    "simulatedWork": "Marisol talks about the four empty desks. Every time someone leaves, the remaining caseworkers absorb the orphaned files, so the backlog grows, so the job gets harder, so the next person leaves sooner. She has watched it spiral for three years. The pay is part of it; a trained caseworker can make more at the distribution center off the highway, with no one crying at their window. But it is not only pay. It is the moral weight of telling people who need help that they have to come back, again, because of a document. \"You go home carrying that,\" she says. \"Most people can only carry it for so long.\"\n\nTheo asks the question that turns a sympathetic conversation into a useful one. \"When a new hire does stay, what makes the difference?\" Marisol answers immediately: the ones who survive are the ones who got real training and got paired with a veteran for their first months. The ones who get thrown onto the floor with a login and a procedures binder are gone by spring. You write that down too, because it is a second lever sitting right next to the first: throughput is not only about the verification process, it is about whether the office can keep enough trained people to run that process at all.\n\nYou leave the break room with something you did not have on the drive out: the shape of the real problem. Eastwood is slow because of a reinforcing loop. Manual verification creates pending stacks and second trips, which makes the work grinding and emotionally heavy, which drives turnover, which removes trained staff, which makes the verification backlog worse. Every consulting instinct in you wants to draw that loop on a page, and you will, but right now you just sit with how clearly Marisol laid it out, having lived inside it for eleven years.",
    "commentary": "You just learned the difference between a problem and a system. A junior analyst reports \"Eastwood is slow due to manual verification and understaffing,\" two separate bullets. A consultant who listened in that break room reports a reinforcing loop where verification friction and staff turnover feed each other, which means fixing only one will not hold. That systems view is what earns the right to a senior person's attention, and you did not get it from data. You got it from forty-five minutes of genuinely listening to someone with no incentive to perform for you. One more thing worth feeling: this conversation had real emotional weight, and good consultants do not armor themselves against that. They let it inform the work. The recommendation you build should be measured by whether it makes Marisol's Monday better, not only whether the median drops.",
    "after": "You thank Marisol in a way you mean. Wanda finds you for a last ten minutes before you drive back, and this conversation is sharper, because Wanda wants to know whether you actually understood what you saw."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-dil-d1-b5": {
    "before": "Wanda gives you the last slot of the visit. She has watched firms walk her floor before. The unspoken question in her face is simple: are you the fourth firm that leaves, or something else.",
    "simulatedWork": "You do not show her the dataset. Instead you tell her what you saw, in plain terms, and let her correct you. \"Here is what I think is happening, and tell me where I am wrong. Your throughput is not slow because your people are slow. It is slow because almost nothing can be finished in one visit, so applications cycle through a pending stack, and each cycle costs days. The verification steps that force those cycles are mostly manual, and the one that hurts most, income, could be done electronically against wage data the state already holds. And the whole thing is made worse by turnover, because every caseworker you lose dumps their files onto the ones who stay.\"\n\nWanda is quiet for a second, and something in her posture changes. \"That,\" she says, \"is the first time one of you has said it back to me right.\" She adds the thing you could not have known: she proposed electronic income verification to headquarters two years ago. It went nowhere, not because anyone disagreed, but because it required two agencies to share data, which required a governance agreement nobody wanted to own, which required political cover that never materialized. The fix was never technical. It was organizational.\n\nYou write that down hard, because it just reframed your entire workstream. The highest-leverage fix is known, has been known for two years, and is stuck not on analysis but on cross-agency governance and political will. That is a very different recommendation than \"here is a clever new idea.\" It is \"here is the fix your own people identified, and here is how to finally unstick it.\" Wanda walks you to the door. The waiting room is full now, every chair taken, a few people standing. She does not have to say anything about it. You both see it.",
    "commentary": "This is the payoff of running the visit Theo's way. Because you led with curiosity and reflected back what you genuinely understood, Wanda gave you the single most valuable fact of the day: the best fix is already known and is stuck on governance, not analysis. That changes your recommendation from invention to advocacy, and it changes your role from outsider with ideas to ally amplifying what the agency's own people have been saying for years. That is often the truest form of value a consultant adds in government: not a novel insight, but the structure, evidence, and outside credibility to finally move something the insiders could never move alone. Note too that you have now triangulated: the data (Eastwood is slow), the caseworker (pending-stack cycle, electronic verification), and the manager (the fix is stuck on cross-agency governance) all point the same direction. Three independent sources agreeing is when a hypothesis becomes a finding.",
    "after": "You and Theo get back in the car. Neither of you talks for the first few miles. Then the work reasserts itself: you have a finding forming, and Renata is expecting a readout. You open the laptop."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-dil-d1-b6": {
    "before": "Back at a borrowed desk in the DHS headquarters annex, you have ninety minutes before you debrief with Renata. The job now is to take what you saw in one office and test it against the statewide dataset, because one office is a story and the data is what makes it a finding.",
    "simulatedWork": "You start with the hypothesis Eastwood handed you: throughput is killed by the pending-stack cycle, driven by manual verification and worsened by turnover. Now you interrogate the data to see if the pattern holds across all 38 counties or whether Eastwood is a special case.\n\nYou build three quick views. First, you correlate each county's median processing time against its caseworker vacancy rate. The relationship is strong and clean: the more chronically understaffed an office, the slower it runs. Second, you look at the fast counties to understand what they do differently, and a pattern jumps out, the fastest offices are the ones that already use electronic data-matching for income verification, quietly, having set it up through local relationships the rest of the state never replicated. That is the smoking gun: the fix Marisol named is not theoretical, it is already working in the counties that have it. Third, you size it. If the slowest two-thirds of counties matched the verification approach of the fastest, you estimate the statewide median could fall from the high 30s into the teens, which would mean the agency could actually absorb the doubled workload coming in 14 weeks instead of collapsing under it.\n\nYou are careful with the estimate. You note your assumptions, you flag that the fast counties may differ in other ways you have not controlled for, and you resist the urge to make the number prettier than it is. Renata has taught you that a defensible smaller number beats an impressive fragile one every time, especially in a setting where a skeptical career deputy will pressure-test every figure.",
    "commentary": "This is the move that turns a field visit into consulting. The floor gave you the hypothesis; the data tells you whether it generalizes, and here it does, three independent ways: vacancies predict slowness, the fast counties already use the fix, and the sizing shows the fix is big enough to matter for the deadline. Notice the intellectual honesty in how you handled the estimate: assumptions stated, confounders flagged, the number kept defensible rather than impressive. That discipline is what lets your work survive contact with a hostile question, and in government, where the career staff have seen consultants oversell before, an honest number is also a trust-building number. You walked the floor to find the truth and you ran the data to prove it. Neither alone would have been enough.",
    "after": "You have a tight story: a known fix, already working where it exists, big enough to save the agency from the coming wave, stuck only on cross-agency governance. Renata is waiting in the small conference room. Time to see if it holds up to her."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-dil-d1-b7": {
    "before": "Renata has thirty minutes between client meetings. She wants the headline from the field day and your read on what it means for the steering committee at the end of the week. This is where you find out whether your day in Eastwood produced a finding or just a nice story.",
    "simulatedWork": "You give her the answer first, the way she has trained you. \"The throughput gap is mostly explained by manual income verification and staffing, and the two reinforce each other. The highest-leverage fix is electronic income verification against state wage data. It already works in the fastest counties. If we scaled it, the median could drop enough to absorb the federal workload increase. And the reason it has not scaled is not technical, it is a cross-agency data-governance problem that has been stuck for two years.\"\n\nRenata pushes, the way she always does. \"How confident are you in the sizing?\" You walk her through your assumptions and your caveats honestly, including what you could not control for. She nods; the honesty is the right answer. Then she asks the harder question. \"If the fix is known and stuck on governance, what do we actually recommend? We cannot put 'two agencies should get along' on a slide.\" You think, and offer: the recommendation is not the idea, it is the unsticking. Lay out the specific governance agreement needed, name who has to own it, quantify the cost of the status quo against the deadline so the commissioner has the political ammunition to force it, and give the career deputy a plan detailed enough that he trusts it will actually work. Renata almost smiles. \"That is the engagement. You just described our deck.\"\n\nShe closes with coaching. \"Good field day. You did the most important thing, which is you came back with the voice of the floor, not just numbers. When we present, Marisol's line about clearing her stack by Friday is going to do more work than your regression. But here is the thing to get right: Frank, the career deputy, has heard consultants pitch shiny fixes before. Our credibility with him depends on making it clear this was his agency's idea first, and we are here to help land it. Lead with respect for what they already knew. Do that and we have a shot at building something that survives after we leave.\"",
    "commentary": "Sit with Renata's last point, because it is the whole philosophy of doing this well. The finding is strong, but its survival depends entirely on how it is positioned to the career deputy who will own it after you are gone. Pitched as \"the brilliant consultants figured out your problem,\" it dies on contact with his pride and his experience. Pitched as \"your own people identified this fix, here is the evidence that it works and the structure to finally unstick it,\" it has a chance to outlive the engagement. That is the difference between a deck that gets filed and a change that actually reaches the line outside that office door. Notice also how Renata coached: she validated what you did right, pushed your rigor, then aimed you at the human and political reality that determines whether any of it matters. That is what good management in this work looks like.",
    "after": "You have a finding, a story, and a clear job for the rest of the week: turn Eastwood into the spine of the steering committee deck. As you pack up, you think about the line outside that brick building, full again by the time you left. The median is a number on your screen. The line is the reason the number matters. You try, as Theo said, not to forget it."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d0-b1": {
    "before": "Sunday evening, your apartment. The weekend was real: you slept in, saw friends, stayed off the laptop. But after dinner the week starts pulling at you, the way it does for almost everyone in this work. Tomorrow you fly to the Hartwell state capital, and Thursday afternoon you stand in front of a commissioner and a 25-year career deputy to present the first real findings of Project Gateway. The whole week points at that room.",
    "simulatedWork": "The ritual takes shape around 8 PM, half logistics, half mental gear-shifting.\n\nThe suitcase first. By now it nearly packs itself: four days of client-appropriate clothes, the toiletry kit that never gets unpacked, chargers for both phones. A carry-on, always. But there is a public-sector wrinkle to the packing you did not have on commercial projects: your laptop and your badge. DHS issued you a temporary access credential after a background check that took three weeks to clear, and you have learned not to leave it in the hotel. Government data has rules, and the rules are not suggestions.\n\nThen the calendar scan. Monday: 6:40 AM flight, war room at DHS HQ by 9, the throughput dataset all afternoon. Tuesday looms in your mind more than the others: a 90-minute drive out to Eastwood County, one of the worst-performing offices in the state, to see the backlog with your own eyes. Thursday in bold: STEERING COMMITTEE, 2:00 PM. The week is a funnel pointing at it.\n\nAt 9:30 your work phone buzzes. Renata, to the team channel: a refreshed county dataset and one line. \"Worth a look before tomorrow. Nothing required tonight.\" You appreciate the second sentence. Not every manager writes it. You skim the column headers for ten minutes anyway, because some part of your brain has already flown to the capital, then close the laptop.\n\nYou think, briefly, about the difference between this and the commercial projects your friends are on. They are optimizing a company's margin. You are trying to make a state process benefits fast enough that fewer families wait three months for food assistance. The hours are the same. The weight of it feels different, and you have not decided yet whether that difference makes it heavier or lighter.\n\nLights out at 10:45. Alarm: 4:55.",
    "commentary": "The Sunday-night feeling is one of the most universal and least advertised parts of consulting, and it does not spare the public sector. Notice the small public-sector textures already showing up: the badge, the background check, the data rules. Government work wraps the familiar consulting week in a layer of access control and compliance that commercial work does not have, and you learn to treat it as part of the job rather than friction to resent. Notice too the thought you had about weight. Many people are drawn to public-sector consulting precisely because the stakes feel human in a way a profit study does not, and many others find that same weight wearing over time. Neither reaction is right or wrong. It is data about you, and this week will give you more of it.",
    "after": "Sleep comes slower than it should. Tomorrow: the flight, the war room, and a dataset of 38 counties with a five-fold mystery hiding inside it."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d1-b1": {
    "before": "The 4:55 alarm, the dark ride to the airport, the 6:40 flight. It is week 4, and the Monday routine is calcifying into muscle memory. Half the gate looks like you: quarter-zips, roller bags, laptops out, the great Monday migration of consultants to their client cities. The difference on a government project is where you are migrating to: not a gleaming corporate campus, but a state office building from the 1970s.",
    "simulatedWork": "Security is fast; you have status now, a strange artifact of this job. At the gate you find Theo and claim the seat next to him. The unwritten team rule, imported from Renata's first engagement: no work talk before wheels-up. So boarding is spent arguing about whether a burrito is a sandwich, which gets unreasonably heated.\n\nIn the air, laptop open, you take a first pass at the refreshed county dataset. You are not building anything at 30,000 feet on hostile wifi; you are orienting. 38 counties, two years of weekly data, processing times by program and stage. You sketch a decomposition on a notepad: total processing time equals time-to-first-touch plus time-in-pending plus time-to-final-determination. If the median is 38 days statewide and the best county is 9, the gap is almost certainly hiding in one of those buckets. By the time the wheels touch down you have a plan for which bucket to open first.\n\nThe landing ritual: phone off airplane mode, the buzz of accumulated messages. A text from your sister, a calendar invite from Renata, and one email from the Commissioner's chief of staff to the whole team that you decide Renata should interpret first. The chief of staff writes in the clipped, urgent register of political offices everywhere: the Commissioner wants to know Thursday what the team will commit to, in numbers, before the federal deadline.\n\nYou and Theo rent a car and drive to DHS headquarters, a long concrete building with a flag out front and a security desk inside. Your badge works. You are in.",
    "commentary": "The rhythm here will feel familiar from any consulting orientation: out Monday, back Thursday, the same flights week after week. A few public-sector notes. First, the client environment is different in a way that matters more than it sounds. A government building has a different pace and culture than a corporate tower, and reading that culture is part of the job. Second, watch the chief-of-staff email. In a company, urgency usually flows from a single CEO. In an agency, urgency often flows from the political layer (the Commissioner and her staff) and lands on you through intermediaries, while the career staff who actually run operations may feel that urgency very differently. You are already, on Monday morning, caught between two clients with two clocks. Learning to serve both is the week's quiet challenge.",
    "after": "War room check-in at 9. Renata is already there, laptop open, the week mapped on a whiteboard. The engagement officially begins."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d1-b2": {
    "before": "The team's war room is a borrowed conference room in the DHS annex, with a long table, a temperamental projector, and a whiteboard Renata has claimed. The week's plan starts here, and Renata plans it the way good managers do: backward from the deliverable.",
    "simulatedWork": "Renata draws the funnel on the board. Thursday, 2 PM: Steering Committee. Audience: Commissioner Reyes and Deputy Delgado, plus a few program directors. The ask: present the first real diagnosis of why throughput varies, and commit to a direction that can move the numbers before the federal deadline. Everything this week serves that meeting.\n\nShe works backward. To present Thursday, the deck must be drafted by Wednesday night and pre-wired with Frank Delgado before the room, because a recommendation the career deputy hears for the first time in the meeting is a recommendation he will resist in the meeting. To draft the deck Wednesday, the finding must be solid by Wednesday morning, which means Tuesday's county field visit has to convert your data hypothesis into something real you have seen with your own eyes. And to make Tuesday count, today has to nail the analysis: where, precisely, the five-fold gap lives.\n\nThen she assigns. Theo owns the systems angle: what the current eligibility software can and cannot do, and what the separate modernization vendor is building. You own county operations: the throughput decomposition, the driver analysis, and the field visit. David, the partner, will be in Wednesday and Thursday, and will handle the Commissioner directly. Renata holds it all together and will pre-wire Frank with you.\n\nShe closes with the thing that separates a good plan from a list of tasks. \"Remember who we are talking to Thursday. Reyes wants a number and a headline she can act on before the deadline. Frank wants to know we actually understand his operation and are not about to embarrass his people. Those are different audiences in the same room. Our deck has to land for both, or it lands for neither.\"",
    "commentary": "Planning backward from the deliverable is one of the most transferable habits in consulting, and you are watching it done well. But notice the public-sector layer Renata added at the end. In commercial work you usually orient to one decision-maker. Here she named two audiences with different needs sitting in the same room, the political appointee who wants a decisive number and the career operator who wants to be understood and respected, and she made serving both the explicit design constraint for the whole week. That dual-audience awareness is the central craft of government consulting, and a junior who grasps it early is worth a great deal to a team.",
    "after": "The week has a shape. You take your half of the whiteboard, find a desk, and open the dataset. The five-fold mystery is yours to crack before tomorrow."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d1-b3": {
    "before": "Three hours, a borrowed desk, and 38 counties of weekly processing data. The job is to turn \"processing times vary a lot\" into a precise statement about where and why, so tomorrow's field visit knows exactly what to look for.",
    "simulatedWork": "You start with the decomposition you sketched on the plane: total time equals time-to-first-touch, plus time-in-pending, plus time-to-final-determination. You build it out county by county, and within twenty minutes the picture sharpens. Time-to-first-touch is roughly similar everywhere. Time-to-final-determination, once everything is in hand, is also similar. The entire five-fold gap lives in one bucket: time-in-pending. The slow counties are not slower at doing the work. They are slower at getting applications out of the pending pile, where they sit waiting on a missing document or a verification that cannot be completed.\n\nThat reframes everything. The question is no longer \"why are some counties slow,\" it is \"why do applications get stuck in pending in some counties and not others.\" You go hunting for what correlates with high pending time. You test caseworker vacancy rate first, because understaffing is the obvious suspect, and the relationship is strong: the more chronically understaffed an office, the longer applications sit. But it does not explain everything. There is a second pattern you cannot fully see in the data yet, something about how each county handles verification. The fastest counties seem to clear pending faster than their staffing alone would predict, as if they have a shortcut.\n\nYou flag the income-verification step as your prime suspect, because it is the verification most likely to require a document the applicant does not have on hand, the kind of thing that sends an application to pending for weeks. But you cannot confirm it from headquarters. The data tells you where the gap lives (pending) and one driver (staffing), and points you at a second you will have to go see (verification). You write the open question on a sticky note: \"Why do the fast counties clear pending faster than staffing predicts? Verification shortcut?\" That sticky note is tomorrow's mission.\n\nYou resist the urge to over-conclude. You have a strong partial answer and a sharp question, which is exactly what a field visit is for. A weaker analyst would walk into Eastwood tomorrow with a vague sense that \"things are slow.\" You will walk in knowing precisely that you are there to understand the pending pile and the verification step, which means you will see in two hours what an unfocused visit would miss in two days.",
    "commentary": "This is the analytical heart of a consulting week, and notice the discipline. You did not try to answer everything from the data. You used it to localize the problem with precision (the gap is entirely in time-in-pending) and to identify one confirmed driver (staffing) and one hypothesis to test in the field (verification). That is the correct division of labor between desk and field: the data tells you where to look and what to look for, and the field tells you why. Notice also the restraint on the estimate and the conclusions. Walking into a client's operation with a confident wrong theory is worse than walking in with a sharp question, because the client can tell the difference, and in government, where the career staff have watched consultants oversell before, intellectual honesty is also a trust strategy.",
    "after": "You have a localized problem and a mission for tomorrow. It is 1 PM and you have not eaten. The team is grabbing lunch before the first real client touchpoint of the week: a working session with Frank Delgado's operations team, where you will meet the man himself."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d1-b4": {
    "before": "The afternoon's working session is with Frank Delgado and two of his program managers. On paper it is a data-gathering meeting. In reality it is your first chance to make an impression on the single most important person to the engagement, a 25-year career civil servant who has watched firms like yours come and go, and who decides whether your work lives or dies after you leave.",
    "simulatedWork": "Frank is exactly as advertised: courteous, careful, and unmistakably skeptical. He has a way of listening to a consultant that makes clear he is waiting to find out whether you are worth his time. Renata opens by asking him to walk the team through how county operations actually work, and you watch a small masterclass in how to treat a career expert: she asks, she listens, she does not perform expertise she has not earned.\n\nWhen your turn comes, you do not lead with your analysis. You lead with a question that shows you did the work. \"We are seeing that the entire processing-time gap between fast and slow counties lives in time-in-pending, not in the actual determination work. Does that match what you see from your seat?\" Frank pauses, recalibrates slightly, the way a skeptic does when a consultant says something true. \"It does,\" he says. \"Most people who come in here think my caseworkers are slow. They are not slow. They are buried.\" He gestures at the idea of the pending pile as if it is a physical weight on his whole operation, which it is.\n\nYou ask the question your sticky note demanded. \"Some counties seem to clear pending faster than their staffing would predict. Is there something different about how they handle verification?\" Now Frank looks at you for a second longer than before. \"There is,\" he says. \"A few counties set up a way to verify income electronically, against the state wage database, instead of waiting for the applicant to bring in a pay stub. It clears pending dramatically. I have wanted to do it statewide for years.\" He stops there, and something in how he stops tells you there is a reason it has not happened, a reason that is not technical. You do not push. You note it, hard, and you have the good sense to let the career expert decide when to tell you the rest.\n\nThe meeting ends with Frank fractionally less skeptical than it began, which at this stage is a real win.",
    "commentary": "This is the most important relationship management of the week, and you handled the opening well. The instinct to lead with your cleverest analysis is strong and wrong. Leading with a question that demonstrates you did the work, and then genuinely listening, is how you earn the right to a career expert's candor. Frank just handed you the central thread of the entire engagement, the electronic verification fix, and the fact that it is stuck on something non-technical, because you treated him as the authority he is rather than a data source to extract. Note what you did not do: you did not push for the reason it is stuck. A skeptic gives you the next piece when he is ready, and forcing it would have cost you the trust you just started building. Patience with a career civil servant is not passivity. It is strategy.",
    "after": "You leave the session with the engagement's spine in hand: the fix exists, it works, and it is stuck on a reason Frank has not yet shared. Tomorrow in Eastwood you will see the pending pile that fix would clear. But first, the afternoon has one more thing: the firm's AI tool, and a stack of other states' approaches to benchmark."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d1-b5": {
    "before": "Renata wants the Thursday deck to show the Commissioner that Hartwell is not alone, that other states have faced the same backlog and that some have solved it. That means benchmarking. You have an afternoon and the firm's internal AI assistant, which is either a superpower or a trap depending on how you use it.",
    "simulatedWork": "The firm's AI assistant can search decades of the firm's prior public-sector work in seconds, draft synthesis, and check a document against house standards. You ask it to pull what the firm knows about benefits-eligibility throughput across states, and in under a minute it surfaces three prior engagements, a benchmark of processing times, and a clean summary of which states adopted electronic data-matching for income verification and what happened to their pending queues. Work that would have taken a junior two days of digging a few years ago lands in front of you before your coffee is cold.\n\nYou feel the familiar double edge. The tool just saved you a day, and you are sharply aware that the day it saved was exactly the kind of grunt research that used to justify hiring a lot of juniors. You also catch it being confidently wrong: it cites a processing-time figure for a peer state that does not match the underlying source when you check, and presents it with the same calm certainty as everything else. So you do what the good second-years told you to do: you treat it as a brilliant, fast, occasionally unreliable research assistant whose every number you verify before it goes anywhere near a client deck. The synthesis is a starting point, not an answer.\n\nBy 5:30 you have a verified benchmark: three peer states that adopted electronic income verification and cut their pending queues substantially, with real figures you have checked against primary sources. It will become a single, powerful slide on Thursday: Hartwell's problem is common, and the fix is proven.",
    "commentary": "Every major firm now runs internal AI tools like this, and using them well is quickly becoming part of the junior craft. Hold onto two honest truths from this block. First, the productivity is real, and so is its implication: the tool compresses exactly the research-and-synthesis work that the junior role was historically built around, which is part of why the shape of that role is changing toward judgment, field work, and client skills, the things the tool cannot do. Second, the tool is confidently wrong often enough that verifying its output against primary sources is not optional, especially before anything reaches a government client who will be making decisions about public money. The consultant of value here is not the one who can prompt the tool, it is the one who knows which of its answers to trust and which to check.",
    "after": "You have your benchmark slide in raw form. It is past 5:30, and the team is breaking for dinner. Theo suggests the team grab food together, which on a consulting engagement is never only about food."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d1-b6": {
    "before": "The team finds a restaurant near the hotel. Team dinners on an engagement are part meal, part decompression, part the quiet apprenticeship where juniors learn how the career actually works from the people one rung up. Tonight Theo, your second-year lifeline, ends up next to you, and you take the chance to ask the things you cannot ask in the war room.",
    "simulatedWork": "The dinner starts as it should, with no work talk: travel disasters, the hot-dog-is-a-sandwich debate revived and somehow escalated, Renata telling a story about her first engagement that makes her seem briefly human. Then, as these dinners do, it drifts toward the work and the life.\n\nYou ask Theo the question you have been carrying. \"Does the public-sector practice feel different from the commercial side, career-wise?\" He thinks about it. The work is different, he says: slower clients, longer engagements, a different kind of satisfaction. But the firm machinery is the same. The same up-or-out model, the same semi-annual reviews where partners sort everyone into performance brackets, the same truth that staffing luck and sponsorship matter alongside raw talent. He is honest about it: a meaningful share of any entering class does not make the two-year promotion, and pretending otherwise helps no one. He also tells you the thing the recruiting materials never quite say, that the people who build careers here are the ones who make their good work visible to people with weight, not as politics layered on top of the job but as part of doing the job well.\n\nThen he says the thing you will remember. \"The reason I stayed on the public-sector side is the day you are about to have tomorrow. On a commercial project, the best day is when the client's margin goes up. Here, the best day is when you watch a caseworker who has been drowning realize you actually understand what is drowning her. That conversation tomorrow in Eastwood is going to do more for you than any model. Watch how you feel during it. That feeling is the whole answer to whether this is your work.\"",
    "commentary": "Two layers worth carrying out of this dinner. First, the practical one: the firm's career machinery (up-or-out, semi-annual review brackets, the role of visibility and sponsorship) runs the same whether your clients are companies or governments, and Theo's honesty about it is the kind of inside knowledge that team dinners exist to transmit. Second, the deeper one: Theo just told you to treat tomorrow's field visit as a fit test on yourself, to watch your own reaction to the human core of the work. That is exactly right. The analysis can be learned by anyone disciplined. Whether the human weight of public service energizes you or wears you down is the thing only you can find out, and the simulation is built to let you find it cheaply, now, before the stakes are real.",
    "after": "Back to the hotel by 9:30. You set out clothes for an early start; Eastwood is a 90-minute drive and you want to be there before the office opens. You think about Theo's line, that tomorrow's conversation will do more than any model. You suspect he is right. Alarm: 5:45."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d2-b1": {
    "before": "Today is the day the dataset becomes real. You and Theo are driving 90 minutes out to Eastwood County, one of the worst-performing offices in the state, to see the pending pile with your own eyes and confirm the verification hypothesis from your sticky note. The numbers told you Eastwood is slow. They did not tell you why. That is the entire point of today.",
    "simulatedWork": "Theo drives. You spend the first twenty minutes walking him through exactly what you are bringing into the room: the whole five-fold gap lives in time-in-pending, staffing explains part of it, and you suspect the rest is income verification. He nods, then gives you the unwritten rules for a government field visit. \"We are guests, not auditors. Wanda, the office manager, has had three firms walk her floor in eight years and watched all of them leave without changing a thing. She will assume we are the fourth. Our whole job this morning is to be the team that actually listened.\" He pauses. \"And do not lead with the dataset. If you walk in and say 'your office is the slowest in the state,' the conversation is over. She knows her numbers. She wants to know if you understand why.\"\n\nYou close the laptop for the last half hour. The capital's office parks give way to farmland, then to a county seat that has seen better decades: a shuttered department store, a courthouse, and a low brick building with a line of people already formed outside the door at 8:20, forty minutes before it opens.",
    "commentary": "Notice what Theo did, because it is the craft of a field visit compressed into a car ride. He reframed you from auditor to guest, told you to lead with curiosity rather than the damning number, and made the human relationship the priority over the analysis. That is not softness, it is effectiveness. In a government office where a career manager has watched consultants come and go, your data is worthless until the person trusts you enough to explain what is behind it. And that line outside the door before opening is your first real data point of the day, and it is in no spreadsheet.",
    "after": "You park across from the brick building. The line is thirty people now, some with kids, a few in folding chairs they brought from home. Theo cuts the engine. \"That line is what we are actually here about. Try not to forget it when we are back arguing about medians.\" You go in."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d2-b2": {
    "before": "Wanda Pruitt, who has run this office for 19 years, meets you at a side door before the public entrance opens. She is brisk, polite, and visibly braced for another firm's visit. She gives you fifteen minutes to walk the floor before the doors open at 9, which you understand is both a courtesy and a test.",
    "simulatedWork": "The office is smaller and more worn than you pictured. Eleven caseworker desks, most piled with leaning stacks of manila folders. A waiting area with maybe twenty chairs, which you already know will not be close to enough. A single intake window, a back room with filing cabinets, and a fax machine that is, astonishingly, running.\n\nYou do what Theo coached: you ask questions and you shut up. \"Walk me through what happens when someone in that line reaches the front.\" Wanda does, and the picture assembles fast. An applicant arrives, often having taken unpaid time off hourly work, often for the second or third time because something in their paperwork was incomplete. Eligibility requires verifying income, household size, residency, and now, under the rules coming in 14 weeks, work hours. Each verification can require a document the applicant does not have, which means another trip, which means the application sits in a stack marked pending for days or weeks.\n\nYou ask how many of her eleven desks are staffed today. Seven. Four positions are vacant; she has been trying to fill them for a year, and the people she hires leave within months for jobs that pay more and do not involve being yelled at by desperate people. Turnover, you realize, is not a footnote to the throughput problem. It may be the throughput problem, tangled together with the verification one.\n\nAt 9:00 the doors open and the room fills. You step aside and watch the first twenty minutes of intake. It is quieter than you expected, and somehow harder to watch for it.",
    "commentary": "This is the heart of why field visits exist. The dataset said \"Eastwood: slow, mostly in pending.\" The floor says seven of eleven desks staffed, paper-based verification, applicants making multiple trips, chronic turnover. The number is the symptom; the floor is the disease. Notice you are forming the systems picture (verification friction and turnover feeding each other) but holding it loosely, as input, not conclusion. You have seen one office. One office is an anecdote until the data confirms it generalizes. The discipline is to let the floor sharpen the hypothesis and the data confirm or kill it.",
    "after": "Wanda hands you off to Marisol, a veteran caseworker who has agreed to let you sit beside her and to talk on her break. This is where the abstraction fully dissolves."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d2-b3": {
    "before": "Marisol has worked this office for eleven years and processes more applications than anyone on the floor. Wanda quietly told you she is the reason Eastwood is not even worse. You pull a chair beside her desk, notebook out, and mostly watch.",
    "simulatedWork": "The first thing you notice is how much of her job is not eligibility judgment but document chasing. A young mother's application has been pending three weeks, waiting on a pay stub the system requires to verify income. Marisol calls, gets voicemail, leaves a message she has clearly left before. \"She works two jobs. She has been in twice. The pay stub she brought was the wrong month. It is nobody's fault and it is going to take her another trip.\" She flags the file and moves it back to the pending stack, the tallest on her desk.\n\nYou count silently. In the first hour Marisol touches maybe fifteen files and completes three. The other twelve go back to pending, each waiting on a document, a callback, a system timeout, or a verification that cannot finish today. You understand the median in your gut now. It is not that the work is slow. It is that almost nothing can be finished in one sitting, so everything cycles through pending multiple times, and each cycle adds days.\n\nYou ask the question you have been holding. \"If you could change one thing about how this works, what would it be?\" She thinks for a real moment. \"Let me verify income electronically. The state has the wage data. It is sitting in another agency's system. If I could check a box and pull it instead of waiting for a woman who works two jobs to find the right pay stub and come back a third time, I would clear half my pending stack by Friday.\" She says it flatly, like someone who has said it before to people who nodded and left. You write it down word for word. Theo, two desks away, catches your eye and gives the smallest nod. You both heard the same thing: the person doing the work just named the highest-leverage fix in the building, and it is not a framework. It is data-matching the state could already do.",
    "commentary": "Two things to carry out of this block. First, the frontline worker is almost always the cheapest, richest, most overlooked source of insight in the whole engagement. Marisol just handed you a finding no dashboard would surface: the pending cycle is the throughput killer, and electronic income verification could collapse it. Second, watch how you took the quote verbatim. In two days, in a steering committee room, that line from a real eleven-year caseworker will land harder than any chart. The data proves the pattern; the voice from the floor makes a commissioner feel it. You need both.",
    "after": "Marisol's break is at 11. She has agreed to spend it telling you and Theo what the last few years have actually been like, the conversation you most need and least want to rush."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d2-b4": {
    "before": "You and Theo sit with Marisol in a cramped break room. No notebook out at first, just listening. This is the conversation that will not show up in any model but will quietly shape everything you recommend.",
    "simulatedWork": "Marisol talks about the four empty desks. Every time someone leaves, the remaining caseworkers absorb the orphaned files, so the backlog grows, so the job gets harder, so the next person leaves sooner. She has watched it spiral for three years. Pay is part of it; a trained caseworker can earn more at the distribution center off the highway, with no one crying at their window. But it is not only pay. It is the moral weight of telling people who need help that they have to come back, again, over a document. \"You go home carrying that. Most people can only carry it for so long.\"\n\nTheo asks the question that turns sympathy into something useful. \"When a new hire does stay, what makes the difference?\" Marisol answers immediately: the ones who survive got real training and got paired with a veteran for their first months. The ones thrown onto the floor with a login and a binder are gone by spring. You write that down too, because it is a second lever sitting right next to the first: throughput is not only about the verification process, it is about whether the office can keep enough trained people to run that process at all.\n\nYou leave the break room with the shape of the real problem: a reinforcing loop. Manual verification creates pending stacks and second trips, which makes the work grinding and heavy, which drives turnover, which removes trained staff, which makes the verification backlog worse. Every consulting instinct wants to draw that loop on a page, and you will. Right now you just sit with how clearly Marisol laid it out, having lived inside it for eleven years.",
    "commentary": "You just learned the difference between a problem and a system. A junior reports \"Eastwood is slow due to verification and understaffing,\" two bullets. A consultant who listened reports a reinforcing loop where verification friction and turnover feed each other, which means fixing only one will not hold. That systems view earns a senior person's attention, and you got it from listening, not data. One more thing worth feeling: this conversation had real weight, and good consultants do not armor themselves against that. The recommendation you build should be measured by whether it makes Marisol's Monday better, not only whether the median drops.",
    "after": "You thank Marisol in a way you mean. Wanda finds you for a last ten minutes, and this conversation is sharper, because she wants to know whether you actually understood what you saw."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d2-b5": {
    "before": "Wanda gives you the last slot of the visit. She has watched firms walk her floor before. The unspoken question in her face is simple: are you the fourth firm that leaves, or something else.",
    "simulatedWork": "You do not show her the dataset. You tell her what you saw, in plain terms, and let her correct you. \"Here is what I think is happening, and tell me where I am wrong. Your throughput is not slow because your people are slow. It is slow because almost nothing can be finished in one visit, so applications cycle through pending, and each cycle costs days. The verification that hurts most, income, could be done electronically against wage data the state already holds. And the whole thing is made worse by turnover, because every caseworker you lose dumps their files onto the ones who stay.\"\n\nWanda is quiet for a second, and her posture changes. \"That is the first time one of you has said it back to me right.\" Then she adds the thing you could not have known: she and Frank both proposed electronic income verification to the state two years ago. It went nowhere, not because anyone disagreed, but because it required two agencies, DHS and the labor department that holds the wage data, to share data, which required a formal data-governance agreement nobody wanted to own, which required political cover that never materialized. The fix was never technical. It was organizational and political.\n\nYou write that down hard, because it just reframed the entire engagement. The highest-leverage fix is known, has been known for two years, works where it exists, and is stuck not on analysis but on cross-agency governance and political will. That is a very different recommendation than \"here is a clever new idea.\" It is \"here is the fix your own people identified, and here is how to finally unstick it.\" Wanda walks you to the door. The waiting room is full now, every chair taken, a few people standing. Neither of you has to say anything about it.",
    "commentary": "This is the payoff of running the visit Theo's way. Because you led with curiosity and reflected back what you genuinely understood, Wanda gave you the single most valuable fact of the engagement: the best fix is already known and is stuck on governance, not analysis. That changes your recommendation from invention to advocacy, and your role from outsider with ideas to ally amplifying what the agency's own people have said for years. That is often the truest value a consultant adds in government: not a novel insight, but the structure, evidence, and outside credibility to finally move something the insiders could never move alone. Note too that you have now triangulated, the data, the caseworker, and the manager all point the same way. Three independent sources agreeing is when a hypothesis becomes a finding.",
    "after": "You and Theo get back in the car. Neither talks for the first few miles. Then the work reasserts itself: Renata is expecting a readout, and you have a finding forming. You open the laptop."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d2-b6": {
    "before": "Back at a borrowed desk in the DHS annex, you have the afternoon to test what you saw in one office against the statewide dataset, because one office is a story and the data is what makes it a finding. Then Renata wants the readout.",
    "simulatedWork": "You interrogate the data against Eastwood's hypothesis. You build three quick views. First, you correlate each county's pending time against its caseworker vacancy rate: strong and clean, the more chronically understaffed, the slower. Second, you look at the fast counties to understand their shortcut, and the pattern jumps out, the fastest offices are the ones already using electronic data-matching for income verification, set up through local relationships the rest of the state never replicated. That is the smoking gun: the fix Marisol named is not theoretical, it is already working where it exists. Third, you size it. If the slowest two-thirds of counties matched the verification approach of the fastest, you estimate the statewide median could fall from the high 30s into the teens, enough that the agency could actually absorb the doubled workload coming in 14 weeks instead of collapsing under it.\n\nYou are careful with the estimate: assumptions stated, confounders flagged (the fast counties may differ in other ways you cannot fully control for), the number kept defensible rather than impressive. Renata has taught you that a defensible smaller number beats an impressive fragile one, especially when a skeptical career deputy will pressure-test every figure.\n\nAt 3:30 you give Renata the headline first, the way she trained you. \"The gap lives entirely in pending. It is driven by manual income verification and staffing, and the two reinforce each other. The highest-leverage fix is electronic income verification against state wage data. It already works in the fastest counties. Scaled, it could drop the median enough to absorb the federal increase. And the reason it has not scaled is a cross-agency data-governance problem that has been stuck for two years.\" Renata pushes on your sizing; you walk her through assumptions and caveats honestly, and the honesty is the right answer. Then she asks the hard one. \"If the fix is known and stuck on governance, what do we actually recommend? We cannot put 'two agencies should get along' on a slide.\" You think, and offer: the recommendation is not the idea, it is the unsticking. Lay out the specific governance agreement, name who must own it, quantify the cost of the status quo against the deadline so the Commissioner has the ammunition to force it, and give Frank a plan detailed enough that he trusts it will work. Renata almost smiles. \"That is the engagement. You just described our deck.\"",
    "commentary": "This is the move that turns a field visit into consulting. The floor gave you the hypothesis; the data tells you it generalizes, three ways: vacancies predict slowness, the fast counties already use the fix, and the sizing shows it is big enough to matter for the deadline. Notice the honesty on the estimate, assumptions and confounders flagged, the number defensible rather than impressive. That discipline is what lets your work survive a hostile question, and in government an honest number is also a trust-building number. And notice Renata's reframe: the recommendation is not the clever idea, it is the structure to unstick a fix the client already knew. That is the engagement.",
    "after": "You have a finding, a story, and a clear job for tomorrow: turn Eastwood into the spine of the deck. As you pack up, you think about the line outside that brick building, full again by the time you left. The median is a number on your screen. The line is why it matters."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d3-b1": {
    "before": "The rookie instinct is to open the slide software and start making pages. Renata banned it. Before a single slide, you build the storyline: the logical spine the whole deck hangs on. Get this right and the slides almost build themselves. Get it wrong and you produce forty beautiful pages that do not add up to an argument.",
    "simulatedWork": "You work in a plain document, no formatting, just the logic. You use the answer-first structure the firm drills into everyone: lead with the conclusion, then support it. The top-line answer: Hartwell can absorb the coming federal workload increase by scaling electronic income verification statewide, a fix already proven in its own fastest counties, but doing so requires unsticking a cross-agency data agreement that has stalled for two years.\n\nThen you build the pyramid beneath it. The answer rests on three supporting arguments, each of which becomes a section. One: the throughput problem is specific and diagnosable, the entire gap lives in time-in-pending, driven by manual verification and staffing. Two: the fix is known and proven, electronic verification already cuts pending dramatically in the fast counties and in peer states. Three: the only thing standing between today and the fix is governance, a data-sharing agreement between DHS and the labor department, which the Commissioner has the authority to force and the deadline gives her the reason to.\n\nUnder each argument you list the evidence: the decomposition, the vacancy correlation, the fast-county comparison, the Eastwood field visit, the peer-state benchmark, the sizing. You can see the deck now without having drawn a single slide. Eastwood is the emotional spine running through all three: the line outside the door, Marisol's pending stack, her line about clearing it by Friday.",
    "commentary": "This is the most underrated skill in consulting, and you are watching it done right. Storylining before slide-building forces the logic to hold before you spend hours on formatting, and it is the difference between a deck that argues and a deck that merely presents. The answer-first, pyramid structure (conclusion on top, supported by a small number of arguments, each backed by evidence) is the house style of the whole profession because it is how busy executives actually absorb a recommendation. Notice you also placed the human thread deliberately. A government deck that is all charts persuades the analyst in the room and loses the Commissioner. Eastwood running through the spine is what makes the logic land for both audiences Renata named.",
    "after": "The storyline is tight. Now the team gathers to pressure-test it before you commit hours to slides, and the partner, David, is in today."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d3-b2": {
    "before": "David Osei, the partner, is in for the first time this week. The team gathers around the whiteboard to war-game the recommendation before it becomes a deck. David spent part of his career inside government before consulting, and he uses every minute of that experience in the next three hours.",
    "simulatedWork": "You walk the storyline. David listens, then starts cutting. He likes the spine. He does not like how soft the recommendation gets exactly where it matters most. \"You have diagnosed beautifully and you have found the fix. Then on the governance slide you go vague, because it is the hard part. That is exactly the slide the Commissioner cares about. Make it the sharpest one.\" He pushes you to specify: which two agencies, what specific agreement, who signs it, what the Commissioner personally has to do, and by when, mapped against the 14-week deadline. The recommendation cannot be \"improve cross-agency governance.\" It has to be a dated, owned, specific play.\n\nHe war-games the room. \"Reyes is going to love the headline and immediately ask what it costs and how fast. Have the number and the timeline ready.\" Then he turns to the harder figure in the room. \"Frank is going to test whether you actually understand his operation or just visited one office and extrapolated. When he pushes, do not get defensive. Show him you saw the system, not just the symptom. And give him the credit, because this was his idea before it was ours.\" David makes you rehearse the answer to Frank's hardest likely question, the one about whether one field visit can really justify a statewide recommendation, until your answer is calm and grounded in the triangulation you actually did.\n\nBy the time the session breaks, the recommendation has an edge it did not have at 9:30. The governance slide is now the sharpest in the deck, with names, dates, and a specific agreement. The soft spot is gone.",
    "commentary": "This is what a good partner adds, and it is worth studying. David did not rewrite your work; he found the exact place where you went vague because the truth was hard, and he made you sharpen precisely there. Junior consultants reliably soften the most important and most difficult recommendation, because specifics feel risky. The discipline is the opposite: the harder and more consequential the recommendation, the more specific it must be. Notice also how much of the session was war-gaming the people in the room, anticipating the Commissioner's cost-and-speed question and Frank's is-this-justified challenge. Anticipating the audience's reactions and rehearsing the responses is most of what separates a presentation that survives the room from one that gets picked apart in it.",
    "after": "You have a sharpened recommendation and a clear build plan. After a quick lunch, you have an unusual meeting: Frank has asked to talk, alone, and you suspect he is finally going to tell you the rest of the governance story."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d3-b3": {
    "before": "Frank Delgado asked Renata if he could sit down with you and her for an hour. After two years of a fix going nowhere, and after a field visit where you clearly listened, he has decided you are worth telling the whole story to. This is the conversation that turns your governance slide from an outline into the truth.",
    "simulatedWork": "Frank lays it out without bitterness, which somehow makes it land harder. Two years ago he and a handful of county managers proposed electronic income verification. Everyone agreed it would work. It died for a reason that has nothing to do with eligibility and everything to do with how government is wired. The wage data lives in the state labor department, a separate agency with its own leadership, its own lawyers, and its own legal obligations around that data. Sharing it with DHS for verification requires a formal data-sharing agreement: privacy review, legal sign-off on both sides, a governance structure to oversee ongoing use. Each piece is doable. None of it has an obvious owner, because it sits between two agencies and belongs fully to neither. And without a senior official willing to spend political capital to force it through, it simply sat. Three commissioners came and went. The fix waited.\n\nHe tells you the part that matters most for Thursday. \"Reyes is the first commissioner in years with both the deadline and the motive to actually push this. The deadline gives her cover. If your deck shows her that this fix solves her backlog problem and shows her exactly what she personally has to do to unstick it, she might finally spend the capital. That is the only way this moves. Not another analysis. A reason and a path for the one person who can force it.\" You realize Frank is not just informing you. He is recruiting you. After two years, he sees an outside team with the Commissioner's ear as the lever he never had, and he is handing you the precise argument that might finally work.",
    "commentary": "This is the moment the engagement's real nature becomes clear, and it is deeply characteristic of public-sector work. The technical fix has been known and agreed for two years. What was missing was not analysis but an owner with the authority and the motive to force a cross-agency agreement through. Notice the alliance forming: Frank, the career deputy who could never move this alone, now sees the consulting team as the instrument to finally move it, and he is arming you with the inside knowledge to do it. That is the highest form of value in government consulting, becoming the credible outside lever that unsticks something the insiders always knew but could never push. And notice it only happened because you earned his trust first. None of this gets told to the firm that walks the floor like an auditor.",
    "after": "You now have the full, true governance story, and a deputy who is on your side. The deck has everything it needs. The rest of the day is the build, and it will run late."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d3-b4": {
    "before": "Storyline locked, recommendation sharpened, governance story in hand. Now the four-hour craft of turning all of it into a deck a commissioner and a deputy can absorb in forty minutes. This is the part outsiders think is the whole job and insiders know is the last ten percent, the ten percent that only works because of everything underneath it.",
    "simulatedWork": "You build answer-first, one message per page, headline selling the point rather than describing the chart. The opening slide states the whole recommendation in two lines, so anyone who reads only one page gets the answer. Then the three sections, each a tight argument. The diagnosis section turns the decomposition into one clean chart: the entire gap is pending, here is the proof. The fix section pairs the fast-county comparison with the peer-state benchmark, the fix works here and elsewhere. The governance section, the one David made you sharpen, is now the most specific in the deck: the exact agreement, the two agencies, what the Commissioner personally signs and convenes, mapped against the 14-week clock.\n\nYou weave Eastwood through all of it. A single photo-free slide carries Marisol's line about clearing half her pending stack by Friday, attributed to an eleven-year caseworker, placed exactly where the logic needs a human to make it real. You run the firm's AI tool over the draft to check it against house formatting standards; it catches two slides with two messages on one page and you split them, because one message per page is not a style rule, it is how the argument stays clean. You verify every number on every slide against its source one more time, because tomorrow a 25-year deputy is going to read each figure like he is looking for the one that is wrong.\n\nBy 7 PM the deck is real: tight, honest, sharp where it matters, human where it counts. Renata reviews it and kills four slides that were showing your work rather than making the argument. The deck gets shorter and better. It usually does.",
    "commentary": "A few things to take from the build. First, the craft is real: one message per page, headlines that sell answers, the human thread placed deliberately, every number verified. Done at this level, slide-building is closer to writing than to formatting, and it is the skill alumni say they use for the rest of their lives. Second, watch Renata cut four slides. The instinct to show all your work is strong and wrong; the deck exists to make an argument, not to prove how hard you worked. Shorter and sharper almost always wins. Third, notice the verification discipline before a government audience. A single wrong number in front of a skeptical career deputy does not just lose that slide, it lets him doubt the whole deck. Accuracy is credibility.",
    "after": "The deck is built and reviewed. There is one thing left tonight that matters as much as the deck itself, and it is not a slide. It is the pre-wire with Frank."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d3-b5": {
    "before": "Renata has taught you an iron rule of high-stakes presentations: the people who matter should never hear the recommendation for the first time in the room. You walk Frank through the near-final deck tonight, the evening before, so that tomorrow he is a partner in the room rather than a critic in it.",
    "simulatedWork": "You and Renata sit with Frank and walk the deck slide by slide. This is not a courtesy, it is strategy. You want his fingerprints on it. When he flags that one figure understates the staffing problem in the rural counties, you fix it on the spot, and now it is partly his slide. When he suggests the governance ask name a specific deputy at the labor department who he knows is sympathetic, you add it, and the recommendation gets more real. Most importantly, you confirm the framing he cares about most: the deck makes unmistakably clear that this fix originated with the agency's own people, that the team's role is to bring the evidence and the structure to finally move it. Frank visibly relaxes as he sees that you are not about to walk into his Commissioner's office and take credit for his idea.\n\nBy the end he is not just comfortable with the deck. He is invested in it. Tomorrow, when the Commissioner asks him directly whether he believes this will work, which she will, he is going to say yes, because it is his idea, validated by an outside team, framed to honor that it was his. That single nod from the career deputy will do more to move the Commissioner than any slide you built.",
    "commentary": "The pre-wire is one of the most important and least visible techniques in consulting, and it is doubly important in government. Walking the key stakeholder through the recommendation the night before turns a potential adversary into an ally and ensures the room has no surprises. But notice the deeper move: you gave Frank ownership. You took his edits, named his contact, and framed the whole recommendation as his agency's idea. That is not manipulation, it is the truth told generously, and it is exactly what makes the work survive after you leave. A recommendation the career deputy owns is a recommendation that gets implemented. A recommendation he merely tolerated dies the day the consultants fly home.",
    "after": "Frank is on your side, the deck is solid, and tomorrow's room holds no surprises for the one person who could sink it. You head back to the hotel late. The presentation is at 2 PM. You go over your two slides one more time before you sleep."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d4-b1": {
    "before": "The Steering Committee is at 2 PM. The morning belongs to rehearsal. A presentation this consequential is never walked into cold; the team runs the deck out loud, anticipates every hard question, and makes sure each person knows exactly which part is theirs. David, the partner, runs the dry run like the room is already watching.",
    "simulatedWork": "The team gathers in the war room. David has everyone present their section out loud, in order, as if Reyes and Delgado were across the table. You present the diagnosis section, the gap lives in pending, the Eastwood story, Marisol's line. David stops you twice. The first time, you are talking too fast because you are nervous; he slows you down. \"You have one job on that slide: make the Commissioner feel the line outside that office before you show her a single number. Let it breathe.\" The second time, he sharpens your handoff into the fix section so the logic flows without a seam.\n\nThen the war-gaming, which is the real point of a dry run. David plays the Commissioner: \"Love it. What does it cost and how fast can I see the median move?\" Renata has the cost and timeline ready. He plays a skeptical program director: \"You visited one county. How do you know Eastwood is not just badly run?\" That one is yours, the exact question David drilled you on yesterday, and you answer it calmly: you did not extrapolate from one office, you triangulated, the statewide data, the field visit, and the deputy's own two-year experience all point the same direction, and the fix is already proven in the fast counties and in peer states. David nods. \"Good. Say it just like that, calm, not defensive. The calm is the credibility.\"\n\nThe last thing David does is confirm the choreography around Frank. At the moment the governance ask lands, David will turn to Frank and ask him directly whether operations believes this will work. Frank, pre-wired and invested, will say yes. That single exchange, the career deputy endorsing the recommendation in front of his Commissioner, is the hinge the whole meeting turns on, and it is staged on purpose because it is true.",
    "commentary": "The dry run is where presentations are won. Notice three things. First, David coached the human beat harder than the analytical one, make the Commissioner feel Eastwood before you show a number, because in a government room the human stakes are what move a political decision-maker. Second, the war-gaming rehearsed the exact hostile questions until your answers were calm and grounded; calm under a hard question reads as credibility, and it is rehearsable. Third, the Frank exchange is choreographed but not fake. It is a true endorsement, staged at the right moment for maximum effect. Orchestrating a presentation so the truth lands with full force is a real and legitimate skill.",
    "after": "The deck is tight, the answers are calm, the choreography is set. There is one more thing before the room: David has a private fifteen minutes with the Commissioner beforehand, and he wants you to come and mostly watch."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d4-b2": {
    "before": "David has a short private meeting with Commissioner Reyes before the full committee convenes. This is partner-level relationship management, and he brings you along to observe, because watching how a partner handles a principal is part of your education that no deck can teach.",
    "simulatedWork": "Reyes is exactly as described: sharp, time-pressured, focused on the deadline and on being seen to act. David does not open with the deck. He opens by demonstrating that he understands her position. \"You have a federal deadline that doubles your workload in 14 weeks, and right now the math does not work. We are going to show the committee a way the math works, and it is a fix your own people have wanted for years. The reason I wanted five minutes first is that the recommendation needs one thing only you can do, and I want you to see it coming so you can decide in the room rather than be surprised by it.\"\n\nYou watch Reyes shift from guarded to engaged in about ninety seconds, because David led with her problem and her agency rather than his firm's cleverness. He previews the governance ask honestly: the fix requires a data-sharing agreement with the labor department that has been stuck for two years for lack of a senior champion, and she is the only person who can be that champion. He does not soften it. He tells her it will cost her some political capital and that the deadline is exactly the cover that makes spending it defensible now. Reyes is quiet, then says, \"Show me the numbers in the room and I will consider it.\" From her, David tells you afterward, that is a green light.",
    "commentary": "This is managing up at the principal level, and it is worth studying closely. David did three things. He led with the Commissioner's problem, not the firm's solution. He pre-wired the single hard ask privately so she would not feel cornered in front of her committee, the same no-surprises principle you used with Frank, applied one level up. And he was honest about the political cost rather than selling a free lunch, which is what builds a principal's trust in an advisor. Notice that a junior could not have run this meeting; it takes the standing and judgment of a partner. But watching it is how you learn what that standing looks like, and someday you will be the one in David's chair.",
    "after": "The committee is assembling in the conference room. Reyes is primed, Frank is ready, the deck is tight. You take your seat. It is 1:55. Showtime."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d4-b3": {
    "before": "The room: Commissioner Reyes at the head, Frank Delgado to her right, three program directors, a representative from the modernization vendor, and your team. Forty minutes. This is the moment the entire week has pointed at, the first time the agency's leadership sees what Project Gateway has found.",
    "simulatedWork": "David opens and frames in two sentences: here is why the backlog exists, here is a proven fix, and here is the one thing this committee can do to make the deadline survivable. Then he hands to you for the diagnosis.\n\nYou remember David's note and let the first slide breathe. Before any chart, you describe the line outside the Eastwood office at 8:20 in the morning, forty minutes before opening, and Marisol's pending stack, and her line about clearing half of it by Friday if she could verify income electronically. The room is quiet in the specific way a room gets quiet when an abstraction becomes a person. Then, and only then, you show the decomposition: the entire five-fold gap between fast and slow counties lives in time-in-pending. You watch Reyes lean in. The human story opened the door; the clean chart walked through it.\n\nRenata takes the fix section: the fastest counties already do electronic income verification, peer states have done it and cut their pending queues substantially, and scaled across Hartwell the median could fall enough to absorb the federal workload increase. Every number is one you verified twice.\n\nThen the governance slide, the sharpest in the deck. The specific agreement, the two agencies, the named sympathetic deputy at labor, and the exact actions the Commissioner personally would take, mapped against the 14-week clock. The ask is precise and owned, not \"improve governance\" but \"here is the agreement, here is who signs it, here is what you convene, by when.\"\n\nDavid lets the slide sit, then turns to Frank. \"Frank, you have run these operations for 25 years. Does your team believe this works?\" Frank does not hesitate. \"It works. We have wanted it for years. What we never had was a way to move it. This is the way.\" The career deputy has just endorsed the recommendation to his Commissioner, in front of her committee. You watch Reyes register it, because his word on operations carries weight hers cannot manufacture.",
    "commentary": "Watch the structure of how that landed. The human story first, then the data, then the proven fix, then the precise ask, then the career deputy's endorsement at the hinge. Each piece was placed where it would do the most work, and the most powerful moment was not a slide at all, it was Frank's two sentences, which you earned on Tuesday by listening and secured last night by pre-wiring. This is the whole craft converging: rigorous analysis made human, sharpened to a specific ask, and validated by the one internal voice the Commissioner cannot dismiss. A junior built the diagnosis. A team built the moment.",
    "after": "The deck is delivered, the endorsement is in the room, and now comes the part no rehearsal fully controls: the questions."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d4-b4": {
    "before": "A government steering committee does not simply receive a recommendation; it tests it. The next thirty minutes are questions, and how the team handles them matters as much as the deck itself. Some of these you rehearsed. One you did not.",
    "simulatedWork": "Reyes goes first, exactly as David predicted: \"What does this cost, and how fast does the median move?\" Renata has it ready, the cost is modest relative to the price of the backlog becoming a crisis, and the median should begin moving within weeks of the agreement, well inside the deadline. Reyes nods, doing the political math in real time.\n\nA program director pushes the question you were drilled on: \"You visited one county. How do you know Eastwood is representative and not just poorly run?\" You answer calmly, the way David coached: you did not extrapolate from one office, you triangulated, the statewide pending data, the Eastwood visit, and Frank's own two-year experience all point the same direction, and the fix is already proven in the fast counties and in peer states. The calm is the credibility. The director sits back, satisfied.\n\nThen the question you did not rehearse, from the vendor representative, with a defensive edge: \"Is this consulting team suggesting our modernization system is not the solution?\" The room tightens. This is a political landmine, the vendor protecting its contract. You do not take the bait, and you watch David handle it the way a partner does, generously and firmly: the new system is essential and complementary, and electronic income verification is exactly the kind of capability it should support; the recommendation is not against the system, it is about unsticking a data agreement the system needs regardless of who built it. The vendor rep relaxes. David turned a threat into an ally in three sentences, by refusing to make it a fight.\n\nReyes closes the questions. She has heard enough. She turns to Frank one more time, a private confirmation in a public room, and Frank gives the smallest nod. She has decided.",
    "commentary": "Three lessons in thirty minutes. First, the rehearsed questions went smoothly precisely because they were rehearsed, the calm answer to \"how do you know\" was built yesterday in the dry run. Second, the unrehearsed one, the vendor protecting its turf, is the kind of political landmine that no analysis prepares you for and that a senior consultant defuses by refusing to make it a fight. Watch how David reframed a perceived attack into a both-and, that instinct to de-escalate rather than win is worth more than being right. Third, notice the role of Frank's nod throughout. In a government room, the career staff's quiet endorsement is the current that actually carries a decision, and you can see the Commissioner drawing on it.",
    "after": "Reyes thanks the team and the committee. She has made a decision, and she is about to say it out loud. The room waits."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d4-b5": {
    "before": "This is the moment that justifies the week. The Commissioner is about to say what she will do, and in government, where so much analysis ends in a deck that gets filed, a clear decision in the room is not guaranteed and never taken for granted.",
    "simulatedWork": "Reyes is direct. She commits to championing the data-sharing agreement personally. She will convene the labor department leadership herself within two weeks, name a senior owner on the DHS side, and use the federal deadline as the forcing function to push the agreement through the legal and privacy reviews on an accelerated timeline. She asks the team to produce the detailed implementation roadmap and to help structure the governance body that will oversee the data use, the next phase of the engagement. She does not pretend it will be easy; she names the obstacles plainly. But she has decided to spend the capital, and she says why in a sentence that lands: \"Families are waiting three months for help my agency is supposed to provide. If the fix has been sitting here for two years, then the failure to move it is mine to correct, and the deadline just took away my excuses.\"\n\nIt is, you realize, exactly what Frank predicted on Wednesday: not another analysis, but a reason and a path for the one person who could force it. The team did not invent the fix. The team gave the Commissioner the evidence, the structure, and the political cover to finally move a fix her own people had identified years ago. That is the win, and it is a more honest and durable kind of win than a clever new idea would have been.\n\nYou think about the line outside the Eastwood office. It will not shorten tomorrow. But for the first time in two years, the thing that would shorten it has a champion with the authority to make it real.",
    "commentary": "Sit with what just happened, because it is the truest picture of value in public-sector consulting. The team did not produce a brilliant novel insight. It took a fix the agency already knew, proved it rigorously, made it human, sharpened it to a specific ask, secured the career deputy's endorsement, and handed the one person with authority both the evidence and the political cover to act. That is not a lesser form of consulting than inventing something new. In government, it is often the higher form, because it is what actually moves. Notice too that the win is honest about its limits: the Commissioner named the obstacles, the work is not done, the roadmap is the next phase. Real recommendations land in a real world that pushes back, and a team that oversold a clean victory would have lost the credibility this honest one earned.",
    "after": "The committee adjourns. Frank catches your eye on the way out and gives you a look that, from a 25-year skeptic, is worth more than any thanks. You have a flight to catch. The work is not over, but the week's hinge has turned the right way."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d4-b6": {
    "before": "The Thursday-evening flight home, the bookend of the consulting week. The adrenaline of the room has drained and left the particular tired that follows a thing that mattered. You have two hours in a middle seat to think about what just happened.",
    "simulatedWork": "You replay the week on the flight. The dataset on Monday that localized the gap to pending. The Tuesday line outside the brick building, and Marisol, and the pending stack. Frank shifting from skeptic to ally across three days. The room today, and the Commissioner's sentence about the failure being hers to correct. You are tired in the way that feels earned.\n\nYou also let yourself notice your own reactions, the thing Theo told you to watch. The part of the week that pulled at you hardest was not the room, impressive as it was. It was the break room with Marisol, the moment a person who had been drowning for years felt understood. The analysis energized you; the human stakes moved you. You file that away as real data about yourself, neither good nor bad, just true, the kind of self-knowledge that the whole exercise of stepping into a career is meant to surface.\n\nA text from Renata lands as the plane taxis: \"Good week. Real win in there, and you carried the diagnosis well. Friday is lighter, we debrief and scope the next phase. Get some rest.\" You put the phone away and watch the lights of the capital fall behind the wing, the city where, somewhere in a building with a flag out front, a Commissioner just decided to spend political capital on a fix that might, eventually, shorten a line of people outside a brick building 90 minutes away.",
    "commentary": "The Thursday flight is the quiet counterpart to the Sunday reset, the week's exhale. Notice what you did with it: you let yourself feel which parts of the week mattered most to you, and you were honest that the human moment moved you more than the executive room. That noticing is the entire purpose of stepping into a simulation like this. The skills are learnable by anyone disciplined. Whether the human weight of public service is the thing that energizes you or eventually wears you is the question only your own reactions can answer, and you just collected a real data point on it, cheaply, before the stakes were yours to live.",
    "after": "Home by 9:30. Tomorrow is a home-office Friday: the debrief, the next-phase scoping, and a career conversation Renata has been meaning to have. The hardest part of the week is behind you. Alarm, mercifully, off."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d5-b1": {
    "before": "Friday from your home office, the gentler end of the consulting week. The team gets on a call to debrief the steering committee, capture what worked, and align on the next phase. Debriefs are where teams actually get better, and good ones are honest about the misses as well as the wins.",
    "simulatedWork": "David runs the debrief crisply. What worked: the human-first diagnosis, the pre-wire with Frank that turned the most important skeptic into the meeting's anchor, and the specific governance ask that gave the Commissioner something she could actually act on. He is generous but precise about your contribution: the Eastwood story carried the room, and you handled the hard question calmly. He is also honest about a miss, the team almost let the vendor-relationship landmine catch them flat, and next time the stakeholder map needs to flag protective parties like the vendor before the room, not in it. Nobody is defensive about it; that is how a good team treats a miss, as a lesson rather than a fault.\n\nThen the forward look. The Commissioner asked for an implementation roadmap and a governance structure for the data-sharing body. That is the next several weeks of the engagement: turning a decision into a plan detailed enough that the labor department's lawyers, DHS operations, and the modernization vendor can all execute against it. Your workstream shifts from diagnosis to design, you will help structure how the electronic verification actually rolls out county by county, sequenced so the worst-backlogged offices get it first.",
    "commentary": "Debriefs are an underrated engine of consulting growth, and notice the culture in this one. The wins are named specifically rather than vaguely, so they can be repeated. The miss, the vendor landmine, is surfaced honestly and turned into a concrete process improvement, flag protective stakeholders before the room, without blame. Teams that cannot debrief their own misses plateau; teams that can, compound. Notice also how your role evolves now, from diagnosis to design. The glamorous part, the room, is over. The part that actually changes the line outside the office, the unglamorous implementation, is just beginning, and it is where a recommendation either becomes real or quietly dies.",
    "after": "The debrief done, Renata asks you to stay on the line for a few minutes. She has the conversation she has been meaning to have, the one about your development."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d5-b2": {
    "before": "Renata stays on after the team drops. This is the conversation no one schedules formally but that quietly shapes a consulting career, an honest read on how you are doing, how the firm's machinery works, and what it takes to keep rising. She is direct, which is a gift.",
    "simulatedWork": "Renata starts with the real feedback, specific and balanced. The strength: you did the most important thing this week, you came back from the field with the voice of the floor, not just numbers, and that human thread is what carried the room. The growth edge: you nearly over-buried the diagnosis slide in detail, and you talked too fast under pressure until David slowed you down. Both fixable, both normal at three months. She wants you working on saying less and trusting the strongest point to carry, the discipline of subtraction.\n\nThen she pulls back the curtain on the machinery, the same honest picture Theo gave you at dinner, now from a manager's seat. The firm runs semi-annual reviews; partners gather feedback from everyone you worked with and sort consultants into performance brackets; the up-or-out model is real, and a meaningful share of any class does not make the next promotion. She tells you the part that is easy to miss: the inputs are not purely mechanical. Staffing luck matters, manager quality matters, and sponsorship matters, having senior people who know your work and will advocate for you in the rooms you are not in. \"That is not politics layered on the job,\" she says. \"It is part of the job. Do excellent work, and make sure it is visible to people with weight. I will sponsor work I have seen. This week, I saw it.\"\n\nThat last line is the one you carry. A manager just told you, in the firm's own language, that she will advocate for you. In a system where sponsorship shapes who rises, that is not a small thing.",
    "commentary": "This conversation models how the career actually advances, and the honesty is the valuable part. Two layers. First, the feedback: specific, balanced, and aimed at a real growth edge, the discipline of subtraction, saying less so the strongest point lands. That is exactly the skill that separates good juniors from great ones. Second, the machinery: semi-annual reviews, performance brackets, up-or-out, and the uncomfortable truth that visibility and sponsorship matter alongside raw talent. Renata framed it correctly, making your good work visible to people with weight is part of doing the job well, not politics bolted on. And she offered sponsorship, which in this system is among the most valuable things a manager can give. Notice it was earned by the work, not asked for.",
    "after": "You have honest feedback, a clear growth edge, and a sponsor. The rest of Friday is lighter: documenting the week and scoping your design workstream for the phase ahead."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d5-b3": {
    "before": "The unglamorous but essential close of the week: capturing what was decided so it does not evaporate, and scoping the design work ahead so Monday starts with momentum rather than a cold start.",
    "simulatedWork": "You write the decision memo, short and precise: what the committee decided, what the Commissioner committed to, the deadlines she named, and the team's deliverables for the next phase. In government, where decisions can blur or quietly reverse, a clear written record of what a principal committed to in the room is its own form of accountability, and worth more than it looks.\n\nThen you scope your design workstream. Diagnosis is done; now comes the harder, less visible work of making the fix real. You sketch the questions the next phase has to answer: in what order do the 38 counties get electronic verification, and how do you sequence it so the worst-backlogged offices, the Eastwoods, go first? What training and staffing support do the counties need so the new capability actually gets used and does not just become another underused feature? How does the governance body oversee the data sharing on an ongoing basis? You are no longer diagnosing a problem; you are designing the path from a decision to a shorter line outside a door. It is less dramatic than the room and, in truth, it is where the real value either gets delivered or lost.",
    "commentary": "This block is a quiet lesson about where consulting value actually lives. The steering committee was the visible peak, but the implementation design you are scoping now is where a recommendation becomes a result or becomes another filed deck. Notice the decision memo, in government especially, writing down exactly what a principal committed to is a tool of accountability, because memory and will both fade and the deadline pressure that produced the decision will compete with a hundred other priorities next month. And notice the sequencing instinct, worst-backlogged counties first, which keeps the human stakes at the center of a now-operational problem. The line outside the Eastwood door is still the scoreboard, even when the work has become logistics.",
    "after": "The memo is filed, the next phase is scoped, and the week is nearly closed. There is one last thing, not a task, just a moment to register what the week was actually about."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d5-b4": {
    "before": "The week's work is done. Before you close the laptop, you let yourself sit with the thing underneath all the analysis, the reason any of it mattered. This is not a deliverable. It is the part of the work that does not fit on a slide.",
    "simulatedWork": "You think about the line. Thirty people outside a brick building at 8:20 in the morning, forty minutes before it opened, some with kids, a few in folding chairs they brought from home because they knew the wait. You think about the young mother with two jobs whose application sat in pending for three weeks over the wrong month's pay stub. You think about Marisol, eleven years at that desk, carrying home the weight of telling people who need help to come back again, and her flat, certain sentence: give me electronic verification and I clear half my stack by Friday.\n\nNone of that shortened this week. The line was full again by the time you and Theo drove away on Tuesday. But something did change this week that did not exist last week: the fix that would shorten that line, stuck for two years between two agencies, now has a Commissioner who has committed to forcing it through, with a deadline for cover and a career deputy who believes in it and an outside team to help build it. That is what a week of consulting bought. Not a shorter line yet. A real, credible path to one, where before there was only a known fix going nowhere.\n\nYou let yourself feel that it was worth doing. Then you notice, again, that this, the human stakes, is the part of the work that reaches you most. Not the room, not the model, the line. You have learned something real about the work and something real about yourself, and you did it in a week, before the stakes were ever yours to carry for real.",
    "commentary": "This is the heart the whole simulation was built to let you find. Strip away the dataset, the deck, the steering committee, and what remains is a line of people outside a door who need help that is taking too long to arrive, and a week of work that moved a real fix closer to them. Public-sector consulting offers exactly this trade: slower, more constrained, more political than commercial work, and in return, stakes you can feel. As you finish, notice which parts of this week pulled at you and which exhausted you. That reaction, projected across years, is the truest answer to whether this work is yours, and it is the one answer no salary table, ranking, or coffee chat could ever give you.",
    "after": "You close the laptop. The week is done. The line is still there, somewhere 90 minutes from a capital you flew home from last night, but it is closer to being shorter than it was on Monday, and you had a hand in that."
  },
  "management-consultant-public-sector-full-d5-b5": {
    "before": "The final half hour, the week's true close. A moment to take stock of what a week in public-sector consulting actually was, and to carry the right things out of it.",
    "simulatedWork": "You take stock. In five days you localized a five-fold problem to a single cause, walked the floor of a struggling county office, listened to the people living inside the problem, triangulated a finding three ways, sharpened it into a specific recommendation, won over the most important skeptic in the building, helped a partner secure a principal's decision, and watched a Commissioner commit to a fix that had been stuck for two years. You also learned the firm's machinery from the inside, got honest feedback and a sponsor, and discovered something true about which parts of this work move you.\n\nYou think about the through-line of the whole week. Government consulting kept everything the main orientation promised, the craft, the rigor, the pyramid, the up-or-out, and wrapped it in a different gravity: a client serving the public under a legal mandate, with fixed money, in public view, where the win is measured in a shorter wait rather than a higher margin, and where the fix often runs not through a clever idea but through the patient, political work of unsticking something the insiders always knew. You met the career civil servant who was the real key, the political appointee who held the authority, and the caseworker who held the truth, and you learned that serving all three is the work.\n\nAnd you carry the question every simulation exists to pose: do you want what this work offers, at the pace and price it charges? You do not have to answer it today. You just have to keep noticing, honestly, how the work made you feel. This week gave you the data. The rest is yours.",
    "commentary": "You have completed a full week on a live government engagement. Look back at the range of it: precise analysis and a county floor, a skeptical deputy and a political principal, a steering committee and a break room, a clean chart and a line of people outside a door. Public-sector consulting lives in that range, and so does the question it puts to you. Which of these moments would you want more of? Which would you happily never repeat? Carry those reactions forward. They are the realest thing you can take from a week like this, and gathering them now, cheaply, before the stakes are yours, is the entire point of stepping into a career before you choose it.",
    "after": "You close the week. Somewhere a Commissioner has a deadline and a decision, a deputy has a path he waited two years for, a caseworker has a stack she might finally clear, and you have a clearer sense of whether this is the work you want. Not a bad week. Next week, the design begins."
  }
}

